It is a complete, runnable Java web solution that adds UI + DB on top of the OOP design.

It is a Spring Boot project (Maven), using Spring Data JPA for DB (embedded H2 by default for easy testing)
and Thymeleaf for a simple web UI.

It uses OOP, 1→many Batch→Participant, and runtime polymorphism for notifications (so when a batch starts each participant 
receives a message via an overridden method).


Technologies used:

Spring Boot web app (runable from IDE or command line)

H2 database (easy to switch to MySQL/Postgres)

Thymeleaf UI: create batches, add participants, list batches/participants, start a batch (shows notifications)

JPA entities: Batch, Participant (with inheritance so overridden method works)

Repositories, Services, Controllers

Maven

Java 17+ (works with 11 too if you prefer)

Spring Boot (2.7.x / 3.x — pick whichever your setup supports)

Dependencies: spring-boot-starter-web, spring-boot-starter-thymeleaf, spring-boot-starter-data-jpa, h2


Important design notes

Batch has List<Participant> — 1-to-many JPA mapping.

Participant is a JPA entity with inheritance strategy SINGLE_TABLE.
ZumbaParticipant extends Participant and overrides notifyBatchStart(String batchName) — this demonstrates runtime polymorphism: 
when we iterate participants and call notifyBatchStart, Java dispatches to the subclass implementation.

When a batch is “started” via the UI, the app calls batchService.startBatch(batchId) → triggers notifications 
(for demo we’ll show messages in UI and persist Notification records).

How this demonstrates runtime polymorphism
Participant defines notifyBatchStart(String).

ZumbaParticipant overrides it.

At runtime when batchService.startBatch iterates List<Participant> and calls p.notifyBatchStart(...), the JVM dispatches to ZumbaParticipant.notifyBatchStart — that's method overriding and runtime polymorphism.

Run instructions
Put the sources into a Maven project (package com.example.zumba).

Build & run:

In Eclipse/IntelliJ: Run ZumbaApplication as Java application (Spring Boot).

Or via command line:

bash
Copy
Edit
mvn spring-boot:run
Open browser: http://localhost:8080/

Use UI to create batches, add participants and click Start Batch.

Notifications will be printed to the server console (and you can extend to show on UI or persist a Notification entity).

H2 console (if needed): http://localhost:8080/h2-console

JDBC URL: jdbc:h2:mem:zumba username sa, no password.

Optional extensions:

Persist Notification records (who was notified & timestamp) and display in UI.

Email/SMS integration when batch starts (plug a NotificationSender bean).

Authentication: Teacher/admin roles (teacher only manages assigned batches).

REST API + React/Angular frontend instead of Thymeleaf.

Switch to MySQL or Postgres for persistent production DB (I can provide config).

